1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,540
Two private spacecraft are on their way to the moon to carry out separate missions.

2
00:00:08,539 --> 00:00:17,300
The landers launched January 15 from the American government's Kennedy Space Center in Florida.

3
00:00:18,640 --> 00:00:27,620
The private company SpaceX used its Falcon 9 launch vehicle to fly the landers into space.

4
00:00:27,620 --> 00:00:34,399
The two separated from Falcon 9 about one hour into the flight.

5
00:00:35,520 --> 00:00:42,719
Mission leaders said the launch went exactly as planned with no problems reported.

6
00:00:43,820 --> 00:00:46,719
The trip to the moon will take some time.

7
00:00:47,380 --> 00:00:53,200
One spacecraft is expected to land on the lunar surface in early March,

8
00:00:53,200 --> 00:00:59,079
while the other should touch down in late May or early June.

9
00:00:59,500 --> 00:01:09,540
In February 2024, the first private spacecraft completed the first US moon landing in more than

10
00:01:09,540 --> 00:01:20,439
50 years. The lander, called Odysseus, was developed by the Texas-based company Intuitive Machines.

11
00:01:21,319 --> 00:01:29,040
The spacecraft experienced some technical problems, but was able to carry out several science

12
00:01:29,040 --> 00:01:35,579
experiments before powering down permanently on the moon in late March.

13
00:01:36,299 --> 00:01:45,859
For this current mission, Texas-based Firefly Aerospace developed Blue Ghost, one of the two

14
00:01:45,859 --> 00:01:54,319
landers launched. The other, named Resilience, belongs to Japanese company Ice Space.

15
00:01:55,239 --> 00:02:02,879
Both are designed to collect data and materials to support several planned moon missions,

16
00:02:03,560 --> 00:02:13,000
some including astronauts in coming years. The Blue Ghost lander is targeting a landing site

17
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:23,960
near a volcanic structure called Mons-La Trée. It is a 480 km basin that sits in the northeast

18
00:02:23,960 --> 00:02:34,020
quarter of the near side of the moon. The American Space Agency, NASA, says the two-meter tall Blue

19
00:02:34,020 --> 00:02:44,479
Ghost, is carrying ten NASA science and technology instruments. They aim to gather valuable scientific

20
00:02:44,479 --> 00:02:55,219
data studying Earth's nearest neighbor. The agency said, NASA's Artemis program aims to send

21
00:02:55,500 --> 00:03:03,020
astronauts to the moon for the first time since the Apollo 17 mission in 1972.

22
00:03:04,379 --> 00:03:13,500
The next planned flight in that program is Artemis II, which is set to launch in April 2026.

23
00:03:14,259 --> 00:03:24,060
In that mission, four astronauts will fly NASA's Orion spacecraft more than 400,000 kilometers

24
00:03:24,780 --> 00:03:34,439
on a trip around the moon. Nikola Fox is the Associate Administrator for NASA's Science Mission

25
00:03:34,439 --> 00:03:43,819
Directorate in Washington, DC. She said in a statement, the agency's cooperation with private

26
00:03:43,819 --> 00:03:53,419
companies is a critical part of bringing humanity back to the moon. Fox added that NASA

27
00:03:53,419 --> 00:04:01,680
chose the new experiments partly because of information learned from NASA's Apollo Space Program,

28
00:04:02,240 --> 00:04:11,919
which began in the 1960s. She said the current mission seeks to ensure the safety and health

29
00:04:11,919 --> 00:04:20,959
of our future science instruments, spacecraft, and most importantly, our astronauts on the lunar

30
00:04:20,959 --> 00:04:29,720
surface. Blue Ghost's equipment includes a tool to collect dirt and another to dig a hole

31
00:04:29,720 --> 00:04:38,819
for measuring temperatures below the lunar surface. The spacecraft is also carrying a device built

32
00:04:38,819 --> 00:04:46,660
to measure light reflections to be used with lasers to better measure the distance between Earth

33
00:04:46,660 --> 00:04:55,360
and the moon. In addition, Blue Ghost is carrying instruments to examine the structure and density

34
00:04:55,360 --> 00:04:58,620
of areas beneath the lunar surface.

35
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:06,860
equipment will seek to capture X-ray images of the edge of Earth's magnetic field.

36
00:05:07,620 --> 00:05:15,720
The ice-space lander resilience is carrying an exploring vehicle called a rover to the

37
00:05:15,720 --> 00:05:16,079
moon.

38
00:05:16,959 --> 00:05:25,199
The 5-kilogram rover is designed to collect lunar soil and other materials from the surface.

39
00:05:25,199 --> 00:05:33,319
Resilience is also carrying equipment and instruments to complete several experiments for

40
00:05:33,319 --> 00:05:37,060
Japanese companies and other organizations.

41
00:05:37,560 --> 00:05:46,339
One of the experiments will test an electrolysis device designed to separate water into hydrogen

42
00:05:46,339 --> 00:05:47,639
and oxygen.

43
00:05:48,639 --> 00:05:55,959
Such a device could help future astronauts better use water resources on the moon and

44
00:05:55,959 --> 00:05:57,779
produce rocket fuel.

45
00:05:58,540 --> 00:06:07,000
Other experiments set for the resilience mission include food production tests and the deployment

46
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:10,199
of a deep-space radiation probe.

47
00:06:11,199 --> 00:06:19,439
The instrument is designed to collect detailed measurements of ionizing radiation in space.

48
00:06:20,399 --> 00:06:30,180
NASA has said it is paying $101 million to Firefly for the mission and another $44 million

49
00:06:31,120 --> 00:06:32,220
for the experiments.

50
00:06:33,439 --> 00:06:38,899
Officials from ice-space did not report how much its mission would cost.

51
00:06:39,899 --> 00:06:42,800
It is the second moon mission for ice-space.

52
00:06:43,540 --> 00:06:51,939
During the last one, Japan's Space Agency, Jaxa, successfully launched its slim spacecraft

53
00:06:52,660 --> 00:06:55,699
to the moon in January 2024.

54
00:06:56,519 --> 00:07:04,000
But the lander touched down imperfectly, causing some communication and power problems.

55
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:13,240
However, Japanese space officials reported they had stayed in communication with slim through

56
00:07:13,240 --> 00:07:14,079
late April.

57
00:07:15,240 --> 00:07:22,759
During this time they said the spacecraft was able to collect valuable data about the landing

58
00:07:22,759 --> 00:07:24,899
and surrounding area.

59
00:07:24,899 --> 00:07:27,879
I'm Brian Lynn.

